item.content:encoded:
&lt;p&gt;Free video calls come with strings attached. Zoom ends your meeting at 40 minutes, Google Meet wants everyone on a Google account, and every call runs through somebody else's servers. &lt;strong&gt;Jitsi Meet&lt;/strong&gt; removes all three problems: it is a fully open-source video conferencing platform you can run on your own Ubuntu 24.04 server, with no accounts required for guests and no artificial time limits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This guide installs a production-ready Jitsi Meet server with HTTPS, a locked-down firewall, and optional authentication so strangers cannot start meetings on your server.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What you need
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A VPS or home server running &lt;strong&gt;Ubuntu 24.04 LTS&lt;/strong&gt; (2 GB RAM minimum, 4 GB recommended for real meetings)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A domain or subdomain pointed at your server's public IP, for example &lt;code&gt;meet.example.com&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sudo access&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ports 80, 443, and 10000/UDP reachable from the internet&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Jitsi's own packages officially support Ubuntu 24.04, so the whole install is &lt;code&gt;apt&lt;/code&gt;-based and upgrades arrive like any other package.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Point DNS and set the hostname
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Create an A record for your subdomain before touching the server. The installer and Let's Encrypt both need it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then set the fully qualified domain name on the server itself:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;hostnamectl set-hostname meet.example.com
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Add it to &lt;code&gt;/etc/hosts&lt;/code&gt; so the machine can resolve itself:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;127.0.0.1 localhost
YOUR_SERVER_IP meet.example.com
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Verify with &lt;code&gt;ping "$(hostname)"&lt;/code&gt; — it should print &lt;code&gt;meet.example.com&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Add the Prosody and Jitsi repositories
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Jitsi uses Prosody as its XMPP signaling server, and the Ubuntu repositories ship a version that is too old for features like the lobby. Add both upstream repositories:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;apt update
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;apt &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-y&lt;/span&gt; apt-transport-https curl gnupg2
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;add-apt-repository &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-y&lt;/span&gt; universe

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Prosody repository&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-sL&lt;/span&gt; https://prosody.im/files/prosody-debian-packages.key &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; /usr/share/keyrings/prosody-debian-packages.key
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/prosody-debian-packages.key] http://packages.prosody.im/debian &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;lsb_release &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-sc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt; main"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
| &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo tee&lt;/span&gt; /etc/apt/sources.list.d/prosody-debian-packages.list

&lt;span class="c"&gt;# Jitsi repository&lt;/span&gt;
curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-sL&lt;/span&gt; https://download.jitsi.org/jitsi-key.gpg.key &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
| &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;sh &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'gpg --dearmor &amp;gt; /usr/share/keyrings/jitsi-keyring.gpg'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;echo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"deb [signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/jitsi-keyring.gpg] https://download.jitsi.org stable/"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
| &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo tee&lt;/span&gt; /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jitsi-stable.list

&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;apt update
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


Open the firewall
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Jitsi needs web traffic plus one UDP port for the actual audio and video media:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw allow 80/tcp       &lt;span class="c"&gt;# Let's Encrypt verification&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw allow 443/tcp      &lt;span class="c"&gt;# Web interface and signaling&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw allow 10000/udp    &lt;span class="c"&gt;# Jitsi Videobridge media — the important one&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw allow 22/tcp       &lt;span class="c"&gt;# SSH&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw allow 3478/udp     &lt;span class="c"&gt;# STUN via coturn&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw allow 5349/tcp     &lt;span class="c"&gt;# Media over TCP fallback when UDP is blocked&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw &lt;span class="nb"&gt;enable
sudo &lt;/span&gt;ufw status verbose
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;If the server sits behind a home router, forward &lt;strong&gt;443 TCP&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;10000 UDP&lt;/strong&gt; to the server. Nearly every "I can join but nobody can see or hear each other" report comes down to port 10000/UDP being blocked somewhere.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Install Jitsi Meet
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;apt &lt;span class="nb"&gt;install&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-y&lt;/span&gt; jitsi-meet
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The installer asks two questions:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hostname&lt;/strong&gt; — enter your FQDN exactly as configured in DNS: &lt;code&gt;meet.example.com&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SSL certificate&lt;/strong&gt; — choose the Let's Encrypt option. Only pick self-signed for a quick lab test; browsers will warn about it, and the Jitsi mobile apps flat-out refuse to connect with a self-signed certificate.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The installer detects Nginx or Apache and configures the virtual host for you. If you ever need to issue or renew the certificate manually:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo&lt;/span&gt; /usr/share/jitsi-meet/scripts/install-letsencrypt-cert.sh
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Confirm the videobridge is listening for media:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;ss &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-ulpn&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep &lt;/span&gt;10000
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;You should see a Java process bound to UDP port 10000.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Test with three people, not two
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open &lt;code&gt;https://meet.example.com&lt;/code&gt; and start a room. Here is the detail most guides skip: &lt;strong&gt;test with three participants&lt;/strong&gt;. Two-person calls in Jitsi often run peer-to-peer directly between browsers and can work even when the videobridge is unreachable. The moment a third person joins, media switches to the videobridge — so a broken bridge only shows up in three-way calls. If video dies when the third person joins, your firewall or NAT rules for 10000/UDP are wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Stop strangers from starting meetings
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;By default, &lt;strong&gt;anyone&lt;/strong&gt; who finds your server can create rooms and consume your bandwidth. Lock room creation to registered users while keeping guests able to join:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;/etc/prosody/conf.avail/meet.example.com.cfg.lua&lt;/code&gt;, change the main VirtualHost authentication:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight lua"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;VirtualHost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"meet.example.com"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;authentication&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"internal_hashed"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then add a guest VirtualHost below it:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight lua"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;VirtualHost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"guest.meet.example.com"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;authentication&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"anonymous"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="n"&gt;c2s_require_encryption&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Tell jicofo to require authentication. In &lt;code&gt;/etc/jitsi/jicofo/jicofo.conf&lt;/code&gt;, add inside the top-level &lt;code&gt;jicofo { ... }&lt;/code&gt; block:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight hocon"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;jicofo&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;authentication&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;enabled&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="l"&gt;XMPP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;login-url&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"meet.example.com"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Finally, point the web frontend at the guest domain. In &lt;code&gt;/etc/jitsi/meet/meet.example.com-config.js&lt;/code&gt;, add &lt;code&gt;anonymousdomain&lt;/code&gt; to the &lt;code&gt;hosts&lt;/code&gt; section:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;config&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;hosts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;meet.example.com&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="na"&gt;anonymousdomain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;guest.meet.example.com&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Then create your moderator account and restart everything:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;prosodyctl register admin meet.example.com &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'a-strong-password'&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nb"&gt;sudo &lt;/span&gt;systemctl restart prosody jicofo jitsi-videobridge2
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Now only you can create rooms; anyone with the link can still join as a guest.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Running behind NAT
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two-way calls work but three-way calls fail on a home server? Tell the videobridge its public IP explicitly in &lt;code&gt;/etc/jitsi/videobridge/jvb.conf&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight hocon"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;ice4j&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;harvest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;mapping&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;static-mappings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;local-address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"192.168.1.50"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;public-address&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"YOUR_PUBLIC_IP"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Restart with &lt;code&gt;sudo systemctl restart jitsi-videobridge2&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


When something breaks, read these logs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;/var/log/jitsi/jvb.log&lt;/code&gt; — media and videobridge issues&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;/var/log/jitsi/jicofo.log&lt;/code&gt; — conference focus and room management&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;/var/log/prosody/prosody.log&lt;/code&gt; — authentication and signaling&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Planning meetings over 100 participants? Raise the systemd defaults in &lt;code&gt;/etc/systemd/system.conf&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;DefaultLimitNOFILE=65000&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;DefaultLimitNPROC=65000&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;DefaultTasksMax=65000&lt;/code&gt;), then &lt;code&gt;sudo systemctl daemon-reload&lt;/code&gt; and restart the videobridge.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The payoff
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You now have a video conferencing service with no meeting time limits, no forced accounts for guests, encrypted media on infrastructure you control, and upgrades through plain &lt;code&gt;apt&lt;/code&gt;. Pair it with the other pieces of a self-hosted stack — &lt;a href="https://clinerds.com/article/filux-self-hosted-file-sharing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;your own file sharing with Filux&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://clinerds.com/article/nextcloud-vs-immich" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;photos with Nextcloud or Immich&lt;/a&gt;, and a &lt;a href="https://clinerds.com/article/self-hosted-backup-strategy-3-2-1" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;proper 3-2-1 backup strategy&lt;/a&gt; — and the subscription pile shrinks one service further.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The PHP security team has issued a warning regarding a new remote code execution (RCE) vulnerability that currently lacks an official patch. While the flaw does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; affect all PHP installations, it poses a significant risk to systems running certain &lt;strong&gt;non‑default&lt;/strong&gt; configurations (e.g., custom &lt;code&gt;php.ini&lt;/code&gt; settings that broaden the attack surface).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


The Vulnerability and Mitigation
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;According to the PHP security advisory, the vulnerability allows an attacker to execute arbitrary code on the server when a specially crafted request reaches a PHP process that has vulnerable configuration options enabled. Because a formal patch is not yet available, the PHP security team has shared specific mitigation measures to help developers and system administrators secure their environments in the interim.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Recommended configuration changes&lt;/strong&gt; (apply only if they fit your environment):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;expose_php = Off&lt;/code&gt; – prevents PHP from advertising its version in HTTP headers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;allow_url_fopen = Off&lt;/code&gt; – blocks remote file inclusion via URL wrappers.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;allow_url_include = Off&lt;/code&gt; – disables inclusion of remote PHP files.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;disable_functions = exec,passthru,shell_exec,system,proc_open,popen,curl_exec,curl_multi_exec,parse_ini_file,show_source&lt;/code&gt; – removes high‑risk functions that could be abused.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;open_basedir = /path/to/your/app&lt;/code&gt; – restricts file system access to a specific directory tree.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review any custom &lt;code&gt;auto_prepend_file&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;auto_append_file&lt;/code&gt; directives that could be leveraged.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Developers are urged to:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Audit PHP Configurations:&lt;/strong&gt; Review any non‑default settings, particularly those related to how the PHP engine interacts with the web server.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Apply Official Mitigations:&lt;/strong&gt; Follow the specific configuration changes recommended by the PHP security team to neutralize the attack vector until a version update is released.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


A Growing Trend of Developer‑Targeted Attacks
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This PHP alert arrives amidst a series of security incidents specifically targeting developer infrastructure and supply chains. Recent reports highlight several high‑priority threats that require immediate attention:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LiteLLM Supply Chain Compromise:&lt;/strong&gt; The LiteLLM project's PyPI package and Docker images were recently compromised to distribute info‑stealer malware designed to exfiltrate credentials and environment variables directly from developer environments. &lt;a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/litellm-release-pipeline-hacked-to-distribute-info-stealer-malware/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[Source]&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Railway Environment Variable Breach:&lt;/strong&gt; The cloud platform Railway warned developers to rotate secrets after detecting suspicious activity in systems used to store customer environment variables. &lt;a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/railway-warns-devs-to-rotate-secrets-after-potential-breach/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[Source]&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;UEFI Bootkit Exploits:&lt;/strong&gt; A new bootkit named &lt;strong&gt;BlackX&lt;/strong&gt; has been identified exploiting the "Baton Drop" flaw to bypass Secure Boot, demonstrating that even low‑level system integrity is under active threat in 2026. &lt;a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/blackx-uefi-bootkit-exploits-baton-drop-flaw-to-bypass-secure-boot/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;[Source]&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


Recommended Actions for Engineering Teams
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Given the current threat landscape, software engineers should prioritize the following security hygiene tasks:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rotate Secrets:&lt;/strong&gt; If you use Railway or similar cloud platforms, rotate your API keys, database credentials, and environment variables immediately.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Verify Dependencies:&lt;/strong&gt; Audit your &lt;code&gt;requirements.txt&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;composer.json&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;package.json&lt;/code&gt; files and ensure you are using verified, untampered versions of packages like LiteLLM.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Monitor PHP Updates:&lt;/strong&gt; Stay tuned to official PHP security channels for the release of the formal patch for the current RCE vulnerability.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Apply the Mitigations Above:&lt;/strong&gt; Adjust your &lt;code&gt;php.ini&lt;/code&gt; (or per‑pool configuration) according to the recommended settings, then restart the PHP service.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AI Disclosure:&lt;/strong&gt; This article was generated with the assistance of AI. The content is based on security reports and technical disclosures from the sources cited below.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Image credit: &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PHP_Programming.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;File:PHP Programming.pdf — CC BY-SA 4.0&lt;/a&gt; — &lt;a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;license&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/challenges/hacktoberfest-week3-2026-10-19"&gt;Hacktoberfest Open-Source AI Challenge Week 3: Slow and Steady&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted a personal assistant for a small daily problem: I have work tasks and personal tasks, but deciding what to do when is another task in itself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Daylight&lt;/strong&gt; turns a task list into a proposed day. Work and personal tasks can share the schedule, with priorities, estimates, fixed appointments and breaks taken into account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The workflow is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set the hours available and add appointments that cannot move.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write one task per line, or add tasks using a form.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Let a local open-weight model suggest category, priority and duration.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Review those suggestions, then add them to the schedule.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Complete tasks or replan the remaining day when something changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Tasks that cannot fit appear under &lt;strong&gt;For another time&lt;/strong&gt;, with an explanation. The planner keeps the original estimate and respects the end of the day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the “Slow and Steady” idea I wanted to explore: spend less effort juggling the list, while still being honest about how much day is left.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Demo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Demo video:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/18yMAobWiORlU3fwNdyZsS7HDo4Fkqr_L/view?usp=sharing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Watch the Daylight demo on Google Drive&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;During development I tried a mixed list: preparing a challenge entry, packing for a trip, and cleaning a pet’s bowl. The early results exposed useful problems, described below. This was hands-on browser testing, not a formal accuracy evaluation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Source repository:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href="https://github.com/Hornet-Wing/daylight" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Hornet-Wing/daylight&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The original application code is MIT licensed. The project uses plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript, plus a small Node.js static server for local use. No dependency installation or build step is required for the manual planner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From the project directory:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;npm start
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Open &lt;code&gt;http://127.0.0.1:4174&lt;/code&gt;. The AI reader separately downloads its runtime and model when enabled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


How I Built It
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I built Daylight with Codex, starting from the behaviour I wanted: list what needs doing, give it rough priorities, and see a realistic day containing both work and personal tasks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI integration uses &lt;a href="https://webllm.mlc.ai/docs/user/basic_usage.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;WebLLM&lt;/a&gt; and the open-weight &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Qwen2.5 0.5B Instruct model&lt;/a&gt;, through its &lt;a href="https://huggingface.co/mlc-ai/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct-q4f32_1-MLC" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;MLC WebGPU build&lt;/a&gt;. Inference runs in a browser worker.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Give the model a smaller job
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The first version asked the model for a complete task list, including optional start and finish times. Browser testing revealed that it could invent full date-and-time strings even when my notes supplied no times. It also omitted one task.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An earlier error handler hid the useful diagnostic because a worker failure arrived as plain text instead of an Error object. Preserving that text exposed a grammar-initialisation failure, which led to a simpler generation schema.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those failures changed the design.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each original task line is now kept by application code. The model handles one line at a time and returns only:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight json"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"category"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"personal"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"priority"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"should"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nl"&gt;"duration"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="w"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This is an example of the response format, not a benchmark result.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The model does not supply task titles or time constraints. Explicit phrases such as “before 5pm” or “after 09:30” are handled separately. Without a supported time phrase, both optional time fields stay blank. Ambiguous source phrases remain visible for correction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Explicit user metadata takes precedence too. For example:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Personal: pack for a trip, medium priority, 37 minutes
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That preserves the stated 37 minutes even if the model suggests something else.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A later trial exposed another limitation: “week 3” became a three-minute estimate, and packing was classified as Work/High. The current request constrains inferred durations to a menu of valid time blocks and includes clearer classification examples. Those changes do not establish that the model’s judgments are accurate. Category, priority and duration still need review.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Let scheduling code handle the clock
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After review, ordinary code builds the schedule:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Original task lines
→ local model suggests category, priority and duration
→ explicit source metadata overrides guesses
→ person reviews and accepts
→ scheduling code fits tasks around appointments and breaks
→ timeline and explicit overflow
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Tasks are considered by priority, then deadline, then input order. Each receives the earliest uninterrupted slot that fits its full duration and constraints. Fixed appointments stay fixed. Breaks reserve time between tasks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a greedy scheduling heuristic, not a claim of an optimal schedule. It does not split tasks automatically, and a missed deadline can leave a task unscheduled. The interface explains those cases so I can make the tradeoff myself.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


Check the rules separately from model quality
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The current automated suite has &lt;strong&gt;50 passing tests&lt;/strong&gt;, including 120 generated scheduling scenarios.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The checks cover overlapping appointments, task durations, deadlines, work/personal hours, overflow, backups, server responses, visible error messages, preservation of source lines, and rejection of invented model time fields.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The AI contract tests use test doubles. They check what the application requests and accepts; they do not measure real-model accuracy. The development environment also restricted automated browser testing. My browser trials supplied the screenshots and failure reports that drove the changes above.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two community references helped frame the approach:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://dev.to/kohei_wada/building-a-cli-task-manager-that-solves-the-when-to-do-it-problem-taskwarrior-lacked-581n"&gt;Building a CLI Task Manager That Solves the "When to Do It" Problem Taskwarrior Lacked&lt;/a&gt;, by &lt;a href="https://dev.to/kohei_wada"&gt;@kohei_wada&lt;/a&gt;, describes transparent scheduling from priorities, estimates and daily capacity.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://dev.to/claupttools/stop-ai-from-inventing-facts-in-document-extraction-496b"&gt;Stop AI From Inventing Facts in Document Extraction&lt;/a&gt;, by &lt;a href="https://dev.to/claupttools"&gt;@claupttools&lt;/a&gt;, discusses keeping missing facts unknown and checking proposed values against their source.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why Does Open Innovation Matter?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A daily task list can mix work information with personal routines. Local inference is useful because the notes can be processed on my device, without sending them to a hosted inference API.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The browser still fetches runtime and model files when needed. This is not a claim that the app makes no network requests. The first model load needs a substantial download and compatible WebGPU hardware; cached files may reduce later downloads.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open weights and an open runtime also let me inspect the integration, change the prompt or experiment with a different model. The scheduling rules are separate, so changing the model does not mean trusting it to rewrite the clock.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Daylight stores its plan in one browser, with JSON backup and restore. There is no account or cloud database. Browser storage is not encrypted, and backups include notes. Calendar sync and multi-device sync are outside this prototype.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The Lazy Payoff
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The intended benefit is less repeated planning: place the tasks once, see what fits, and rebuild the remaining day when plans change.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I have not measured minutes saved or run a sustained daily-use trial. What I have is a working prototype, hands-on feedback, and a clearer understanding of where a small local model needs boundaries and human correction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The next useful measurement is how much I edit its suggestions and how much manual rearranging remains after it proposes a day.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;The code and this article were prepared with Codex assistance.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


52,068 SonarQube pages on 9000, 12,052 fingerprints, and the tokens that sit in the project settings
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;SonarQube is a code analysis platform. Teams connect it to a source repository, it pulls the code, analyses it and reports on quality and security findings. To do that it stores credentials: repository tokens, CI webhook secrets, and in many installs the integration tokens that let the pipeline push analysis results.&lt;br&gt;
Port 9000 is SonarQube's default and is also one of the most crowded ports on the internet, because every tool with a web interface that needed a port picked a round number. A count on that port is a count of a neighbourhood.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


The measurements
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Requested from ZoomEye through the vendor SDK on 26 October 2026, one record per query, scope all asset types. Totals only.&lt;br&gt;
| Query | Reported total |&lt;br&gt;
| --- | --- |&lt;br&gt;
| &lt;code&gt;port=9000 &amp;amp;&amp;amp; service="http"&lt;/code&gt; | 7,869,772 |&lt;br&gt;
| &lt;code&gt;port=9000 &amp;amp;&amp;amp; title="SonarQube"&lt;/code&gt; | 52,068 |&lt;br&gt;
| &lt;code&gt;app="SonarQube"&lt;/code&gt; | 12,052 |&lt;br&gt;
| &lt;code&gt;app="SonarQube" &amp;amp;&amp;amp; country="US"&lt;/code&gt; | 3,948 |&lt;br&gt;
| &lt;code&gt;title="SonarQube" &amp;amp;&amp;amp; country="US"&lt;/code&gt; | 36,298 |&lt;br&gt;
The HTTP count on 9000 is close to eight million and is not worth quoting as anything except a demonstration that a port is not a product. Requiring the SonarQube title cuts it to 52,068, and the application fingerprint gives 12,052.&lt;br&gt;
The direction of the gap between title and fingerprint is the interesting part. Titles outnumber fingerprints by more than four to one. SonarQube's web application sets a page title that names the product on its login page, so a title match is easy. The fingerprint depends on a distinctive response that survives the authentication redirect, and a scanner that only receives a login page has less to work with.&lt;br&gt;
The title count is therefore the better upper bound for reachable instances, and the fingerprint count is the conservative floor. Quoting only one of them, without saying which, produces a number a reader cannot interpret.&lt;br&gt;
The regional figures continue the pattern and follow the same field logic: a country filter applied to a title count inherits the title's looseness, so 36,298 should not be compared with 3,948 as if they measured the same thing on different bases.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Why a reachable SonarQube is worth a ticket
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The platform holds the analysis results for every project it scans, which is a list of every known weakness in the organisation's source code with file and line references. It is a map of where to look, and more useful to an attacker than a generic version fingerprint.&lt;br&gt;
It also holds the tokens. A project's analysis token lets a caller submit analysis results for that project, which can influence the quality gate. A global token or an administrator session does considerably more: user management, project configuration, and the ability to create new tokens.&lt;br&gt;
SonarQube's documentation covers the authentication model and the token permissions. The default for a self-hosted install is form authentication, and the administrator account is created during setup with a password that operators have been known to leave at the default value in a lab and then promote to production.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Checks for your own estate
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Confirm the instance is reachable only from the network that runs the analysis and the developers who read the results. A public instance is a design decision worth recording, and most estates find at least one that nobody intended.&lt;br&gt;
Confirm the default administrator password was changed at setup. This is a one-line check against the identity API and it catches the case the documentation warns about.&lt;br&gt;
Review token permissions and revoke tokens for projects that no longer exist. Stale tokens are the ones that outlive the employee who created them.&lt;br&gt;
Check whether the instance is configured with a forced user authentication default, which determines whether anonymous users can browse project dashboards. Anonymous read is a deliberate feature on public instances and an unnecessary disclosure on internal ones.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


Limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The counts describe pages and fingerprints observed on one day, and a SonarQube behind an SSO proxy that returns a generic redirect will appear in neither. The numbers do not establish that authentication is weak, only that the interface is answering. Regional splits follow address geolocation.&lt;/p&gt;

